{"meta":{"title":"Cottage Folk"},"sections":[{"id":"wrap0000","href":"wrap0000.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"\"Cover\"","navLabel":null,"blocks":[]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-0.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-0.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"Cottage Folk","navLabel":"Cottage Folk","blocks":[{"text":"Transcriber’s Note:","heading":false},{"text":"Blank pages have been removed, and if in the pagination, the page numbers skipped in order to retain…","heading":false},{"text":"The Novel of the Indian Mutiny","heading":false},{"text":"On the Face of the Waters","heading":false},{"text":"By FLORA ANNIE STEEL","heading":false},{"text":"Mrs. Comyns Carr","heading":false},{"text":"London","heading":false},{"text":"William Heinemann","heading":false},{"text":"1897","heading":false},{"text":"All rights reserved","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-1.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-1.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"CONTENTS","navLabel":"CONTENTS","blocks":[{"text":"CONTENTS","heading":true},{"text":"Page","heading":false},{"text":"The Hoppers","heading":false},{"text":"1","heading":false},{"text":"A Retrospect","heading":false},{"text":"201","heading":false},{"text":"A Woman’s Wager","heading":false},{"text":"225","heading":false},{"text":"A Ne’er-do-weel","heading":false},{"text":"251","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-2.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-2.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"THE HOPPERS","navLabel":"THE HOPPERS","blocks":[{"text":"THE HOPPERS","heading":true},{"text":"THE HOPPERS","heading":false},{"text":"The girl didn’t stop.","heading":false},{"text":"Mary Ann was hurrying on, but she turned back.","heading":false},{"text":"“What!” she shrieked. “Who says so?”","heading":false},{"text":"“Well, I never!” she ejaculated.","heading":false},{"text":"Then he took heart of grace—he came close to her.","heading":false},{"text":"“Ye didn’t say that up yonder,” said she.","heading":false},{"text":"Then she sprang to its side, and snatching it to her breast and bending o’er it a face, tender as th…","heading":false},{"text":"“I’ll think on it.”","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-3.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-3.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"A RETROSPECT","navLabel":"A RETROSPECT","blocks":[{"text":"A RETROSPECT","heading":true},{"text":"A RETROSPECT","heading":false},{"text":"Martha laughed harshly.","heading":false},{"text":"The woman looked at him sharply.","heading":false},{"text":"He brought his slow gaze back to her.","heading":false},{"text":"Milly’s eyes shone and she clapped her hands.","heading":false},{"text":"“For me! What, niver!” she murmured.","heading":false},{"text":"But she talked for two.","heading":false},{"text":"“I shouldn’t wonder,” agreed Bill Hewson through his mufflers.","heading":false},{"text":"And then they reached the inn and went in to the warmth, and Martha Hewson saw her husband to bed.","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-4.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-4.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"THE BREAD-WINNER","navLabel":"THE BREAD-WINNER","blocks":[{"text":"THE BREAD-WINNER","heading":true},{"text":"THE BREAD-WINNER","heading":false},{"text":"“Why ain’t ye at work?” repeated his wife.","heading":false},{"text":"He drew his arm away.","heading":false},{"text":"But the humbler Chapel-goer was not daunted.","heading":false},{"text":"The child had come out on to the little terrace.","heading":false},{"text":"And she too stumped down the broken steps.","heading":false},{"text":"“O’ course father were allers fust.”","heading":false},{"text":"And wearily the mother repeated:","heading":false},{"text":"“Yes, father were allers fust.”","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-5.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-5.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"HIS LITTLE MAID","navLabel":"HIS LITTLE MAID","blocks":[{"text":"HIS LITTLE MAID","heading":true},{"text":"HIS LITTLE MAID","heading":false},{"text":"The man looked uneasy.","heading":false},{"text":"“Ain’t no one looking after ’em?” asked he.","heading":false},{"text":"The woman smiled.","heading":false},{"text":"“What, all the way?” asked he.","heading":false},{"text":"She nodded her head.","heading":false},{"text":"“Did I?” said he dreamily.","heading":false},{"text":"Tom Wycombe sat as he had sat three months ago, when he had given thanks to his God for restoring hi…","heading":false},{"text":"And now he understood what was the meaning of the peace which passeth all understanding.","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-6.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-6.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"A FARM TRAGEDY","navLabel":"A FARM TRAGEDY","blocks":[{"text":"A FARM TRAGEDY","heading":true},{"text":"A FARM TRAGEDY","heading":false},{"text":"“I’d sooner know,” she sighed.","heading":false},{"text":"“What’s that there?” she whispered terrified.","heading":false},{"text":"“Where?” he asked, turning to follow her gaze.","heading":false},{"text":"“The barmaid’s gone,” answered the woman.","heading":false},{"text":"It was his last word.","heading":false},{"text":"But Charley Chiswick was dead.","heading":false},{"text":"Jim Preston stood dazed with the letter in his hand. He remembered how he had offered to serve her t…","heading":false},{"text":"He gazed at the message—the useless message from a dead mother and a dead child to the father who ha…","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-7.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-7.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"A BROKEN TRYST","navLabel":"A BROKEN TRYST","blocks":[{"text":"A BROKEN TRYST","heading":true},{"text":"A BROKEN TRYST","heading":false},{"text":"“Who says as they be ungrateful?” said ’Melia.","heading":false},{"text":"Still the man looked at her quietly.","heading":false},{"text":"* * * * * * * *","heading":false},{"text":"It had been a hard fight, and save for one hand that had often been stretched out for her in the dar…","heading":false},{"text":"There had been no time, as she had guessed, for larking, or trysting, or love-making. And it was in …","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-8.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-8.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"AN ONLY SON","navLabel":"AN ONLY SON","blocks":[{"text":"AN ONLY SON","heading":true},{"text":"AN ONLY SON","heading":false},{"text":"The grocer himself interrupted her.","heading":false},{"text":"“Why, I always used to,” he began.","heading":false},{"text":"“When’s the last train to Seacombe?” said he.","heading":false},{"text":"He smiled vaguely.","heading":false},{"text":"Frewin laughed.","heading":false},{"text":"“Ye’ve caught me though,” said he.","heading":false},{"text":"So the wager was won, but never paid.","heading":false},{"text":"For the trip to town was so deftly managed from a neighbouring station in the early hours of the Sun…","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-9.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-9.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"A NE’ER-DO-WEEL","navLabel":"A NE’ER-DO-WEEL","blocks":[{"text":"A NE’ER-DO-WEEL","heading":true},{"text":"A NE’ER-DO-WEEL","heading":false},{"text":"He searched for the place and found it; it was dying of cold and hunger.","heading":false},{"text":"He put it in his warm bosom within his coat; he thought that he would feed it when he got in, and th…","heading":false},{"text":"THE END","heading":false},{"text":"Richard Clay & Sons, Limited, London & Bungay.","heading":false},{"text":"THE MANXMAN","heading":false},{"text":"By HALL CAINE","heading":false},{"text":"In One Volume, price 6s.","heading":false},{"text":"The Bradford Observer.—‘It is a fine piece of art, and should touch its readers to fine issues.’","heading":false},{"text":"The Manchester Courier.—‘The book is most interesting, and embodies a great deal of careful work, be…","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-10.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-10.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"A NE’ER-DO-WEEL","navLabel":null,"blocks":[{"text":"London: WILLIAM HEINEMANN, 21 Bedford Street, W.C.","heading":false},{"text":"THE YEARS THAT THE LOCUST","heading":false},{"text":"HATH EATEN","heading":false},{"text":"By ANNIE E. HOLDSWORTH","heading":false},{"text":"In One Volume, price 6s.","heading":false},{"text":"The plumber’s wife ejaculated[,]","heading":false},{"text":"Added.","heading":false},{"text":"ad.1","heading":false},{"text":"The or[i]ginality of the book","heading":false},{"text":"Inserted.","heading":false}]},{"id":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-11.htm","href":"7696831315902964313_63361-h-11.htm.xhtml","linear":true,"types":[],"docTitle":"THE FULL PROJECT GUTENBERG™ LICENSE","navLabel":"THE FULL PROJECT GUTENBERG™ LICENSE","blocks":[]}]}